\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The unnormalized OTF of a clear circular pupil is the shaded overlap of two frequency disks, each of radius $f_c$. Their centre separation is the intensity frequency $q$. Dividing the overlap by $\pi f_c^2$ gives the values shown. There is no overlap beyond $2f_c$. These disks are in spatial-frequency space, not two physical holes in the optical system.}}{10}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:overlap}{{3}{10}{The unnormalized OTF of a clear circular pupil is the shaded overlap of two frequency disks, each of radius $f_c$. Their centre separation is the intensity frequency $q$. Dividing the overlap by $\pi f_c^2$ gives the values shown. There is no overlap beyond $2f_c$. These disks are in spatial-frequency space, not two physical holes in the optical system}{figure.3}{}}
